{"model": "q35", "prompt": "Below is the source of taskq, a small in-process job queue we use inside a web service, followed by its existing tests and a bug report from the on-call channel.\n\ntaskq.py:\n\n\"\"\"taskq: a small in-process job queue for a web service.\n\nJobs are described by a callable name (resolved through a handler registry),\npositional arguments, and a priority. A Worker polls a JobStore, runs the\nhandler on a helper thread with a timeout, and applies a RetryPolicy when a\nhandler raises or times out.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport enum\nimport itertools\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport random\nimport sqlite3\nimport threading\nimport time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field, replace\nfrom typing import Any, Callable, Iterable, Protocol\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"taskq\")\n\n\nclass JobStatus(str, enum.Enum):\n    \"\"\"Lifecycle states of a job.\"\"\"\n\n    PENDING = \"pending\"\n    RUNNING = \"running\"\n    DONE = \"done\"\n    FAILED = \"failed\"  # failed, will be retried\n    DEAD = \"dead\"  # out of attempts\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Job:\n    \"\"\"A unit of work. Lower priority numbers run first.\"\"\"\n\n    id: int\n    name: str\n    args: list[Any] = field(default_factory=list)\n    priority: int = 10\n    attempts: int = 0\n    max_attempts: int = 3\n    run_after: float = 0.0\n    status: JobStatus = JobStatus.PENDING\n    created_at: float = field(default_factory=time.time)\n    started_at: float | None = None\n    last_error: str | None = None\n\n    def is_runnable(self, now: float) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"True if the job may be claimed at time ``now``.\"\"\"\n        return (\n            self.status in (JobStatus.PENDING, JobStatus.FAILED)\n            and self.run_after <= now\n        )\n\n\nclass JobStore(Protocol):\n    \"\"\"Storage interface used by the Worker and by Stats.\"\"\"\n\n    def enqueue(self, name: str, args: Iterable[Any] = (), *, priority: int = 10,\n                max_attempts: int = 3, delay: float = 0.0) -> Job: ...\n\n    def claim_next(self, now: float) -> Job | None: ...\n\n    def mark_done(self, job_id: int) -> None: ...\n\n    def mark_failed(self, job_id: int, error: str, retry_at: float | None) -> None: ...\n\n    def all_jobs(self) -> list[Job]: ...\n\n\nclass MemoryStore:\n    \"\"\"Thread-safe in-memory store, handy for tests and single-process use.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self._jobs: dict[int, Job] = {}\n        self._ids = itertools.count(1)\n        self._lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def enqueue(self, name: str, args: Iterable[Any] = (), *, priority: int = 10,\n                max_attempts: int = 3, delay: float = 0.0) -> Job:\n        with self._lock:\n            now = time.time()\n            job = Job(id=next(self._ids), name=name, args=list(args),\n                      priority=priority, max_attempts=max_attempts,\n                      run_after=now + delay, created_at=now)\n            self._jobs[job.id] = job\n            return replace(job)\n\n    def claim_next(self, now: float) -> Job | None:\n        with self._lock:\n            ready = [j for j in self._jobs.values() if j.is_runnable(now)]\n            if not ready:\n                return None\n            job = min(ready, key=lambda j: (j.priority, j.run_after, j.id))\n            job.status = JobStatus.RUNNING\n            job.attempts += 1\n            job.started_at = now\n            return replace(job)\n\n    def mark_done(self, job_id: int) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            self._jobs[job_id].status = JobStatus.DONE\n\n    def mark_failed(self, job_id: int, error: str, retry_at: float | None) -> None:\n        with self._lock:\n            job = self._jobs[job_id]\n            job.last_error = error\n            if retry_at is None:\n                job.status = JobStatus.DEAD\n            else:\n                job.status = JobStatus.FAILED\n                job.run_after = retry_at\n\n    def all_jobs(self) -> list[Job]:\n        with self._lock:\n            return [replace(j) for j in self._jobs.values()]\n\n\nclass SqliteStore:\n    \"\"\"SQLite-backed store. One connection, guarded by a lock.\"\"\"\n\n    _SCHEMA = \"\"\"\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS jobs (\n        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n        name TEXT NOT NULL,\n        args TEXT NOT NULL,\n        priority INTEGER NOT NULL,\n        attempts INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n        max_attempts INTEGER NOT NULL,\n        run_after REAL NOT NULL,\n        status TEXT NOT NULL,\n        created_at REAL NOT NULL,\n        started_at REAL,\n        last_error TEXT\n    );\n    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS jobs_ready ON jobs (status, priority, run_after);\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, path: str = \":memory:\") -> None:\n        self._db = sqlite3.connect(path, check_same_thread=False)\n        self._db.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        self._lock = threading.Lock()\n        with self._lock:\n            self._db.executescript(self._SCHEMA)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _to_job(row: sqlite3.Row) -> Job:\n        data = dict(row)\n        data[\"args\"] = json.loads(data[\"args\"])\n        data[\"status\"] = JobStatus(data[\"status\"])\n        return Job(**data)\n\n    def enqueue(self, name: str, args: Iterable[Any] = (), *, priority: int = 10,\n                max_attempts: int = 3, delay: float = 0.0) -> Job:\n        now = time.time()\n        with self._lock, self._db:\n            cur = self._db.execute(\n                \"INSERT INTO jobs (name, args, priority, max_attempts, run_after,\"\n                \" status, created_at) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n                (name, json.dumps(list(args)), priority, max_attempts,\n                 now + delay, JobStatus.PENDING.value, now),\n            )\n            row = self._db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE id = ?\",\n                                   (cur.lastrowid,)).fetchone()\n        return self._to_job(row)\n\n    def claim_next(self, now: float) -> Job | None:\n        with self._lock, self._db:\n            row = self._db.execute(\n                \"SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE status IN ('pending', 'failed')\"\n                \" AND run_after <= ? ORDER BY priority, run_after, id LIMIT 1\",\n                (now,),\n            ).fetchone()\n            if row is None:\n                return None\n            self._db.execute(\n                \"UPDATE jobs SET status = 'running', attempts = attempts + 1,\"\n                \" started_at = ? WHERE id = ?\", (now, row[\"id\"]))\n            row = self._db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM jobs WHERE id = ?\",\n                                   (row[\"id\"],)).fetchone()\n        return self._to_job(row)\n\n    def mark_done(self, job_id: int) -> None:\n        with self._lock, self._db:\n            self._db.execute(\"UPDATE jobs SET status = 'done' WHERE id = ?\", (job_id,))\n\n    def mark_failed(self, job_id: int, error: str, retry_at: float | None) -> None:\n        status = JobStatus.DEAD if retry_at is None else JobStatus.FAILED\n        with self._lock, self._db:\n            if retry_at is None:\n                self._db.execute(\n                    \"UPDATE jobs SET status = ?, last_error = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n                    (status.value, error, job_id))\n            else:\n                self._db.execute(\n                    \"UPDATE jobs SET status = ?, last_error = ?, run_after = ?\"\n                    \" WHERE id = ?\", (status.value, error, retry_at, job_id))\n\n    def all_jobs(self) -> list[Job]:\n        with self._lock:\n            rows = self._db.execute(\"SELECT * FROM jobs ORDER BY id\").fetchall()\n        return [self._to_job(r) for r in rows]\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass RetryPolicy:\n    \"\"\"Exponential backoff with proportional jitter.\n\n    delay = min(cap, base * factor ** (attempt - 1)), then scaled by a random\n    factor in [1 - jitter, 1 + jitter].\n    \"\"\"\n\n    base: float = 1.0\n    factor: float = 2.0\n    cap: float = 300.0\n    jitter: float = 0.25\n\n    def delay_for(self, attempt: int) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Seconds to wait after the given (1-based) failed attempt.\"\"\"\n        raw = min(self.cap, self.base * self.factor ** max(attempt - 1, 0))\n        spread = raw * self.jitter\n        return max(0.0, raw + random.uniform(-spread, spread))\n\n    def next_run(self, job: Job, now: float) -> float | None:\n        \"\"\"Timestamp of the next try, or None when attempts are exhausted.\"\"\"\n        if job.attempts >= job.max_attempts:\n            return None\n        return now + self.delay_for(job.attempts)\n\n\n_REGISTRY: dict[str, Callable[..., Any]] = {}\n\n\ndef handler(name: str | None = None) -> Callable[[Callable[..., Any]], Callable[..., Any]]:\n    \"\"\"Register a function as a job handler under ``name`` (default: its name).\"\"\"\n\n    def decorate(fn: Callable[..., Any]) -> Callable[..., Any]:\n        key = name or fn.__name__\n        if key in _REGISTRY:\n            raise ValueError(f\"handler {key!r} already registered\")\n        _REGISTRY[key] = fn\n        return fn\n\n    return decorate\n\n\ndef get_handler(name: str) -> Callable[..., Any]:\n    \"\"\"Look up a registered handler or raise KeyError.\"\"\"\n    return _REGISTRY[name]\n\n\nclass JobTimeout(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a handler does not finish within its time limit.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass Worker:\n    \"\"\"Polls a store, runs handlers, and records outcomes.\n\n    Each job runs on a daemon thread so a stuck handler cannot block shutdown;\n    Python cannot kill threads, so a timed-out handler keeps running in the\n    background until it returns on its own.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, store: JobStore, *, policy: RetryPolicy | None = None,\n                 poll_interval: float = 0.2, job_timeout: float = 30.0) -> None:\n        self.store = store\n        self.policy = policy or RetryPolicy()\n        self.poll_interval = poll_interval\n        self.job_timeout = job_timeout\n        self._stop = threading.Event()\n        self._thread: threading.Thread | None = None\n\n    def start(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Start the polling loop on a background thread.\"\"\"\n        if self._thread and self._thread.is_alive():\n            return\n        self._stop.clear()\n        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._loop, name=\"taskq-worker\",\n                                        daemon=True)\n        self._thread.start()\n\n    def shutdown(self, wait: float = 5.0) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Ask the loop to stop after the current job; True if it exited in time.\"\"\"\n        self._stop.set()\n        if self._thread is None:\n            return True\n        self._thread.join(wait)\n        return not self._thread.is_alive()\n\n    def _loop(self) -> None:\n        while not self._stop.is_set():\n            if not self.run_once():\n                self._stop.wait(self.poll_interval)\n\n    def run_once(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Claim and execute one job. Returns False if nothing was ready.\"\"\"\n        job = self.store.claim_next(time.time())\n        if job is None:\n            return False\n        try:\n            self._execute(job)\n        except Exception as exc:  # handler failure or timeout\n            self._record_failure(job, exc)\n        else:\n            self.store.mark_done(job.id)\n        return True\n\n    def _execute(self, job: Job) -> None:\n        fn = get_handler(job.name)\n        box: dict[str, Any] = {}\n\n        def target() -> None:\n            try:\n                box[\"result\"] = fn(*job.args)\n            except BaseException as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - re-raised by caller\n                box[\"error\"] = exc\n\n        t = threading.Thread(target=target, name=f\"taskq-job-{job.id}\", daemon=True)\n        t.start()\n        t.join(self.job_timeout)\n        if t.is_alive():\n            raise JobTimeout(f\"{job.name} exceeded {self.job_timeout}s\")\n        if \"error\" in box:\n            raise box[\"error\"]\n\n    def _record_failure(self, job: Job, exc: BaseException) -> None:\n        # TODO: distinguish permanent errors (bad args) from transient ones\n        retry_at = self.policy.next_run(job, time.time())\n        message = f\"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}\"\n        log.warning(\"job %s (%s) failed on attempt %d: %s\",\n                    job.id, job.name, job.attempts, message)\n        self.store.mark_failed(job.id, message, retry_at)\n\n\ndef summarize(jobs: Iterable[Job]) -> tuple[dict[str, int], float | None]:\n    # returns (counts, mean_wait); kept as a tuple for historical reasons\n    counts = {s.value: 0 for s in JobStatus}\n    waits: list[float] = []\n    for job in jobs:\n        counts[job.status.value] += 1\n        if job.started_at is not None:\n            waits.append(max(0.0, job.started_at - max(job.created_at, 0.0)))\n    return counts, (sum(waits) / len(waits) if waits else None)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Stats:\n    \"\"\"Counts by status plus mean seconds between enqueue and first start.\"\"\"\n\n    counts: dict[str, int]\n    mean_wait: float | None\n\n    @classmethod\n    def collect(cls, store: JobStore) -> \"Stats\":\n        counts, mean_wait = summarize(store.all_jobs())\n        return cls(counts=counts, mean_wait=mean_wait)\n\n    @property\n    def total(self) -> int:\n        return sum(self.counts.values())\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        parts = \", \".join(f\"{k}={v}\" for k, v in self.counts.items() if v)\n        wait = \"n/a\" if self.mean_wait is None else f\"{self.mean_wait * 1000:.1f}ms\"\n        return f\"{self.total} jobs ({parts or 'empty'}); mean wait {wait}\"\n\nExisting tests in tests/test_worker.py:\n\nimport time\n\nfrom taskq import JobStatus, MemoryStore, RetryPolicy, Worker, handler\n\n\n@handler(\"echo\")\ndef echo(value):\n    return value\n\n\n@handler(\"boom\")\ndef boom():\n    raise RuntimeError(\"always fails\")\n\n\ndef make_worker(store, **kw):\n    policy = RetryPolicy(base=0.01, factor=2.0, cap=0.05, jitter=0.0)\n    return Worker(store, policy=policy, poll_interval=0.01, **kw)\n\n\ndef test_successful_job_is_marked_done():\n    store = MemoryStore()\n    job = store.enqueue(\"echo\", args=(1,))\n    make_worker(store).run_once()\n    assert store.all_jobs()[0].id == job.id\n    assert store.all_jobs()[0].status is JobStatus.DONE\n\n\ndef test_failing_job_is_retried_then_dead():\n    store = MemoryStore()\n    store.enqueue(\"boom\", max_attempts=3)\n    w = make_worker(store)\n    for _ in range(10):\n        w.run_once()\n        time.sleep(0.06)\n    final = store.all_jobs()[0]\n    assert final.status is JobStatus.DEAD and final.attempts == 3\n\nBug report from the on-call channel, filed Friday:\n\n\"A handler that takes longer than its timeout keeps running in its thread after the worker marks the job as failed and schedules a retry. We saw the same invoice get processed twice, once by the zombie thread and once by the retry. Also, when we shut the service down with SIGTERM, jobs that were claimed but not yet started stay in the RUNNING state forever, and nothing ever picks them up again after a restart.\"\n\nFollow-up comment from Sam, same thread:\n\n\"Possibly related: the SQLite store seems to hand the same row to two workers if we run two processes against one database file. I have not reproduced it reliably. We are not blocked on that, but if the fix you propose touches claim_next anyway, please say whether it matters.\"\n\nConstraints: keep the public API backwards compatible, add no new dependencies, and stay on Python 3.12 with the standard library only. Both SQLite and in-memory stores must keep working, and the fix must not make the common, healthy path noticeably slower. Please do the following. First, explain the root causes of both problems in the bug report, pointing at the specific functions involved. Second, write the fix: give the complete revised code for every function or class you change, including a way to recover jobs that were left in the RUNNING state when a previous process died. Third, write new pytest tests that would have caught each problem. Fourth, describe the remaining risks and what you would monitor in production. Use clear headings, show code in code blocks, and for each change say which failure mode it prevents and whether any caller could notice a difference.\n", "temperature": 0, "max_tokens": 256, "ignore_eos": true, "return_token_ids": true}
